      "writtenOn": "2023-11-07T21:24:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "C / C++ have what is called \"integer promotions\", which basically means that when figuring which type to use when evaluating arithmetic operators, first both operands are \"promoted\" to `int` (unless the range of the operand does not fit in the range of `int`, in which case `unsigned int` is chosen instead, unless that also does not fit, in which case the type is not promoted.)\n\nThis evaluation will take the high four bits of a particular byte, and shift them up (as an `int`), to make them occupy bits 11:8.\n\nhttps://www.geeksforgeeks.org/integer-promotions-in-c/",
      "parentUuid": "e2cbc714_d3b57d7b",
